Z||000001||80's BOY||05-17-2000||03:26 PM||||hey coyote! you sent me the top picture last month for my full targa kit question. i think you'll be in good shape. my car has a 1/4 inch twist to the right in the windshield but it has also been in a minor accident before i got it. other than that, the top comes off and on without trouble. i just need to find out how to keep it on there!! put yours back together, man!! how can you stand to see it sitting there and not drive it?? it must keep you up at night. by the way, i used the partial roll bar method discussed in my earlier thread. seems to be just fine. ||||reg||6
Z||000002||dman||05-17-2000||11:44 PM||||Very cool coyote!<P>I'm curious to know if you will be installing a header -- the end fitting that is used to cover the gap above the windshield where you cut it from the roof.<P>I've seen two fiero convertibles that both used the same type of header. They both fit extremely well and looked like they were designed for the Fiero. I don't know if they were custom made or if they were taken from another car with a similar shape. I've heard of people using Pontiac Sunfire convertible tops on their Fiero/Ferrari355 spyder conversions but the headers that I saw didn't look as perfect as the first two I mentioned.<P>From the pics (and their names) I gather you've put in front and rear supports. Will you be putting in another support underneath? What is the purpose of the front and rear supports, iow, what specifically do they help prevent from flexing/breaking?<P>Once again...cool project you've got their Coyote. Can't wait to see more pictures of it as you progress.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spyder||05-18-2000||12:48 AM||||Coyote,<BR>Looks great and being done right.<BR>I like seeing projects in progress.<P><BR>I’m using the Sunbird header on my car and I will take a good pic of it and post it sometime next week.<P>Here is a pic of the Sunbird header on a Fiero 355:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/355yeltop.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Monkeyman||05-18-2000||10:04 AM||||Spyder--Did those mirrors come with the body kit or what??  Are they electric or manual?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spyder||05-18-2000||11:33 AM||||The mirrors come with the kit.  I’ll see if they can be sold separate. (I want a set for my wife’s car)<BR>They come just bare shell, no mirror at all. If you have power mirrors on your car now, the mirrors have a place to mount the motor. But it would probably be easier (cheaper) to buy some motorcycle mirrors that look similar.||||reg||1
Z||000006||coyote||05-18-2000||04:26 PM||||Dman and Frank:<BR>What i did is at the rear of the unibody i cut out a chunk of the body at the end of the rocker, (2.5"X2.5") or so. there is a bit of material that you will have to remove inside the frsme tube. then i pounded in a 2x2 steel tube all the the way into frame. you should stop when the tube starts to deform the front of the rocker in the front wheel well. It will stretch the rocker but the plastic runner will hide it. I then cut off the tube flush to the body and welded a 1X2 steel tube to it and the body. In the front wheel well a welded a 1X1 steel tube into the well. The idea here is to keep the body from closing up like a clam shell and have my ass drag on the ground. Most installations i have seen do not use the verticle supports, i believe that what i have done further ties the body together. Basically if i welded a tube across the fron and the rear it would be a tubular frame, but i figured this is enough. this car will be a removable hard top so i will make a connector for the roof my self||||reg||1
